    "language_name": "english",
    "short_text": "Al-Baqarah (the Cow) has been so named from the story of the Cow occurring in this Surah (67-73). It has not, however, been used as a title to indicate the subject of the Surah. It will, therefore, be as wrong to translate the name Al-Baqarah into \"The Cow\" or \"The Heifer\" as to translate any English name, say Mr. Baker, Mr. Rice, Mr. Wolf etc., into their equivalents in other languages or vice versa, because this would imply that the Surah dealt with the subject of \"The Cow\". Many more Surahs of the Quran have been named in the same way because no comprehensive words exist in Arabic (in spite of its richness) to denote the wide scope of the subject discussed in them. As a matter of fact all human languages suffer from the same limitation.",
        "source": "Shaykh-ul-Islam Dr Muhammad Tahir-ul-Qadri - Irfan al-Qur’an",
    "text": "<h2>Name</h2>
<p>
Al-Baqara [The Cow]
</p>
<p>
Al-Baqara is the 2nd sūrah of the Qur’ān, which means ‘The Cow’. It takes its name from the story of the Cow in verses <a href="/2/67-71">67–71</a>. It is also known as the Chapter of the Throne, the Peak of the Qur’ān and the Chapter of the Brightness.
</p>

<h2>Period of Revelation</h2>
<p>
It was the first Medinan sūrah, revealed after the migration of the Holy Prophet ﷺ. It is the 87th sūrah in chronological order, revealed after Sūrah al-Muṭaffifīn.
</p>
<p>
It is the longest sūrah of the Qur’ān, comprising 286 verses, 6,121 words, 25,500 letters, and 40 segments.
</p>

<h2>Theme</h2>
<p>
After beginning with three Mysterious Letters of the Qur’ān (<a href="/2/1">1</a>), the sūrah describes attributes of the believers, disbelievers, and hypocrites (<a href="/2/2-20">2–20</a>). It opens with the definition of faith, based on belief in the Unseen without entertaining any doubt, establishing the prayer, giving charity, believing in all sacred books and scriptures revealed to the Holy Prophet Muhammad ﷺ or the Prophets and Messengers before him ﷺ, and having sure and profound faith in Resurrection.
</p>

<p>
This sūrah further details the account of the creation of Prophet Ādam (Adam) عليه السلام (<a href="/2/30-39">30–39</a>), the rebellion of Iblīs, the endeavours of Prophet Mūsā عليه السلام (including the story of the cow <a href="/2/67-71">67–71</a>), Hārūt and Mārūt (<a href="/2/102">102</a>), Prophet Ibrāhīm عليه السلام, and the change of the Qibla from Jerusalem to Mecca (<a href="/2/142-152">142–152</a>).
</p>

<p>
Necessary commands, rituals and acts of worship are legislated in this sūrah, including praying, fasting, pilgrimage, marriage, divorce, inheritance, bequest, jihād, charity, debts, retribution for killing, the Qibla’s direction, etc. (<a href="/2/153-283">153–283</a>)
</p>

<p>
Other legal matters discussed are economic contracts, the status of orphans, causes and conduct of a just war, and the significance of peace and security. This sūrah also contains the commandments regarding the prohibition of ribā, pork, alcohol consumption, gambling and punishment for certain capital crimes.
</p>

<p>
This chapter dedicates a sizeable portion to the practices and attitudes of the Israelites (<a href="/2/40-120">40–120</a>). As for the hypocrites, it describes some of their most pertinent traits, such as lying, deception, plotting, foolishness, mockery of Allah Almighty’s signs, corruption, ignorance and straying from the right path.
</p>

<p>
This sūrah also lifts the prohibition on fighting which had been enforced during the Meccan period, permitting the believers to fight in self-defence without transgressing limits (<a href="/2/190">190</a>).
</p>

<h2>Virtues</h2>
<p>
Regarding the merits and virtues of this sūrah, al-Bukhārī has narrated in al-Ṣaḥīḥ on the authority of Usayd b. Ḥuḍayr رضي الله عنه, who said that he was reciting Sūrah al-Baqara at night while his horse was tied beside him; the horse was suddenly startled. When he stopped reciting, the horse calmed down, and when he restarted, the horse was startled again. Then he stopped reciting, and the horse calmed down (again). The next morning, he informed the Holy Prophet ﷺ, who said: ‘Recite, O Ibn Ḥuḍayr! Recite, O Ibn Ḥuḍayr!’ Usayd b. Ḥuḍayr رضي الله عنه replied: ‘O Messenger of Allah, my son Yaḥyā was near the horse, and I was afraid that it might trample on him, so I raised my head and went to him. When I lifted my head towards the sky, there was something like a cloud containing what looked like lamps. So I went out to see it.’ The Holy Prophet ﷺ said: ‘Do you know what it was?’ Usayd b. Ḥuḍayr رضي الله عنه replied: ‘No.’ The Holy Prophet ﷺ said: ‘They were angels who had come nearer to you for your reciting (the Sūrah of Baqara). If you had kept on reciting it till morning, the people would have observed it, and this scene would not have disappeared from them.’
</p>

<p>
Muslim has mentioned in al-Ṣaḥīḥ, on the authority of Abū Hurayra رضي الله عنه, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘Do not render your homes graveyards. Surely, Satan flees from a home where Sūrah al-Baqara is recited.’ In another narration, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘Do not render your homes graveyards. Surely, Satan does not enter a home where Sūrah al-Baqara is recited.’
</p>

<p>
Some of the Qur’ān’s most famous and abundantly recited verses are mentioned in this sūrah, including the Pedestal Verse (Āyat al-Kursī, <a href="/2/255">255</a>) and the final two verses (<a href="/2/285-286">285–286</a>).
</p>

<p>
al-Tirmidhī narrates from Abū Hurayra رضي الله عنه, who said: ‘The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Everything has a peak, and the peak of the Qur’ān is Sūrah al-Baqara, and it contains a verse that is the chief of all the Qur’ānic verses—it is the Pedestal Verse (Āyat al-Kursī).”’
</p>

<p>
Regarding the last two verses of the chapter, al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrate on the authority of Abū Masʿūd al-Badrī رضي الله عنه who said: ‘The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “The two verses from the end of Sūrah al-Baqara—whoever recites them at night, they will be sufficient for him.”’
</p>"
